# Per-Workspace IAM for Dagster on EKS

## What "per-workspace IAM" means

In Dagster OSS, a *workspace* is the set of **code locations** loaded by the webserver
and daemon. One code location ≈ one project: its own repo, its own image, its own
dependency set.

Per-workspace IAM means each code location runs under its **own AWS identity** instead
of every project sharing the node role or a single cluster-wide service account.

| Without | With |
| --- | --- |
| One role for all runs | One role per project |
| Any op can read any bucket | Team A reads `s3://team-a/*`, nothing else |
| Blast radius = cluster | Blast radius = project |

## The thing that trips people up

A code location is **not one pod**. Three pod types are involved, and IAM has to follow
all of them:

1. **Code server pod** — long-lived gRPC deployment that serves definitions.
2. **Run pod** — created per run by `K8sRunLauncher`.
3. **Step pods** — created per step, only if using `k8s_job_executor`.

Actual data access (IO managers, S3 reads/writes, Secrets Manager, Redshift, etc.)
happens in **run and step pods**, not the code server. Annotating only the code server
service account gives isolation on paper and none in practice.

Step pods inherit their pod spec from the run pod, so the real requirement is:
**run pods must carry the project's service account.**

## How to achieve it

Fits the existing two-chart pattern: platform chart (webserver, daemon, run launcher
defaults) + per-project code location chart, both driven by ArgoCD ApplicationSets.

### 1. One IAM role per project

Trust the specific service account, not the namespace.

- **IRSA** — trust policy conditions on the cluster OIDC provider:
  `sub = system:serviceaccount:<namespace>:dagster-<project>`
- **EKS Pod Identity** — create a pod identity association mapping
  `(cluster, namespace, serviceAccount) -> roleArn`. No OIDC condition string to
  hand-maintain per role; preferred where available.

Keep the policy scoped: project bucket prefix, project secrets path, project KMS key.

### 2. Service account in the per-project chart

```yaml
# templates/serviceaccount.yaml (per-project code location chart)
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: dagster-{{ .Values.project.name }}
  namespace: {{ .Release.Namespace }}
  annotations:
    # IRSA only; omit when using Pod Identity
    eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: {{ .Values.project.iamRoleArn }}
```

Set it on the code server deployment:

```yaml
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: dagster-{{ .Values.project.name }}
```

### 3. Propagate to run pods — the load-bearing setting

```yaml
dagster-user-deployments:
  deployments:
    - name: {{ .Values.project.name }}
      image: ...
      includeConfigInLaunchedRuns:
        enabled: true
```

`includeConfigInLaunchedRuns` copies the code server's pod spec — service account,
env, envSecrets, labels, image pull secrets — onto launched run pods.

**If this is false**, run pods fall back to the platform chart's
`runLauncher.config.k8sRunLauncher.serviceAccountName`, and every project shares one
role again. This is the single most common way per-workspace IAM silently fails.

### 4. Step pods

Inherit from the run pod. No extra config unless a specific op needs a different
identity, in which case override per-op:

```python
@op(tags={"dagster-k8s/config": {
    "pod_spec_config": {"service_account_name": "dagster-<project>-elevated"}
}})
def privileged_op(): ...
```

### 5. Wire it into the ApplicationSet

Drive `project.name`, `project.iamRoleArn`, and the service account name from the same
per-env values file that already triggers deployment. Adding a project should be one
file in the helm-charts repo, not three coordinated edits.

```yaml
# usw2sandbox-values.yaml
project:
  name: team-a
  iamRoleArn: arn:aws:iam::<acct>:role/dagster-team-a-usw2sandbox
```

## Design decisions to settle before building

### Shared infrastructure breaks pure isolation

These are touched by every project and need deliberate handling:

- **Compute log manager bucket** — either grant each role a prefix-scoped write
  (`s3://dagster-logs/<project>/*`) or accept a shared path and document it.
- **RDS / instance storage** — the run pod talks to the Dagster instance DB. That
  credential is platform-level, not project-level.
- **Registry pull creds** (ECR / JFrog) — image pull secrets, not IAM; propagated via
  the same `includeConfigInLaunchedRuns` mechanism.

### Namespace strategy

| Option | Isolation | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Single namespace, one SA per project | IAM boundary only | Low — recommended for MVP |
| Namespace per project | IAM + K8s RBAC + quota | Daemon needs Role/RoleBinding in each namespace; per-location `namespace` config |

Start with single namespace. The IAM boundary is the valuable half and it does not
require the RBAC work.

### IAM vs K8s RBAC

Do not conflate them in the writeup:

- **K8s RBAC** — the daemon's permission to create Jobs/Pods for runs.
- **AWS IAM** — what the workload can reach in AWS once running.

Per-workspace IAM changes the second. The first stays platform-owned.

## Verification

```bash
# confirm the run pod actually got the project SA
kubectl get pod <run-pod> -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'

# confirm the identity inside the pod
kubectl exec <run-pod> -- aws sts get-caller-identity

# negative test — the point of the exercise
kubectl exec <run-pod> -- aws s3 ls s3://<other-team-bucket>/   # expect AccessDenied
```

The negative test is the one that matters. A passing positive test only proves
credentials exist, not that they are scoped.
